+    <section class="journal" id="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-heading">
+      <h2 id="journal-heading">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p class="journal-meta">Notes from the roastery on how we find, buy, and roast our beans</p>
+
+      <div class="journal-body" id="journal-text">
+        <p>
+          Every bag of coffee we sell starts a long way from our roastery, and usually at an altitude where the air is
+          thin and the mornings are cold. Over the past decade we have built direct relationships with eleven
+          producer families and three washing-station cooperatives across Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, and
+          Sumatra. We do not buy through anonymous spot markets. Instead, our green buyer visits each origin at
+          least once a year, walks the plots with the farmers, and agrees on prices before the harvest begins. That
+          early commitment matters: it lets producers plan their labor, their fertilizer purchases, and their
+          processing without gambling on where the commodity price will land in six months.
+        </p>
+
+        <h3>How we choose a lot</h3>
+        <p>
+          Sourcing begins with cupping, and a lot of it. During harvest season our team evaluates somewhere between
+          two and three hundred samples, each roasted to a light, consistent profile so that nothing hides behind
+          roast character. We score on sweetness, acidity, body, and clarity of flavor, but the number we care most
+          about is uniformity across the sample. A lot that tastes brilliant in one cup and flat in the next usually
+          signals uneven drying or mixed cherry ripeness, and it will not hold up over a season on our shelves. We
+          also ask for moisture and water-activity readings on every lot we shortlist, because coffee that leaves
+          origin too wet will fade long before it reaches your cup.
+        </p>
+
+        <h3>Paying for quality, not just volume</h3>
+        <p>
+          Our pricing model is simple and public. We start from the Fairtrade minimum, then add a quality premium
+          that scales with the cupping score, plus a fixed relationship bonus for every producer who has sold to us
+          for three consecutive years. In the most recent harvest our average price paid to producers was a little
+          more than two and a half times the C-market price. We publish these figures in our annual transparency
+          report, along with the FOB price, the importer's margin, and the freight costs, so that anyone can see
+          where the money goes. We think this is the minimum standard for calling coffee "ethically sourced", and
+          we would rather show our work than ask you to take the phrase on faith.
+        </p>
+
+        <h3>Processing and the journey home</h3>
+        <p>
+          We buy washed, natural, and honey-processed coffees, and we work with producers on experimental
+          fermentations when they are interested, but we never push a process that a farm cannot execute
+          consistently with the equipment it has. Once a lot is contracted, it is milled, bagged in GrainPro liners
+          inside jute, and shipped in temperature-monitored containers. When it arrives, we cup it again against the
+          original pre-shipment sample. If a coffee has lost character in transit, we talk with the producer and the
+          importer about what happened rather than quietly blending it away. Then the roasting team spends a week
+          dialing in a profile that highlights what made us fall for the coffee in the first place, and it finally
+          makes its way to the bar, the shelf, and, with any luck, your morning.
+        </p>
+      </div>
+
